---
/**
 * AngaDriveWriteUp.astro
 *
 * The body of the AngaDriveV3 report. Sits inside the route's window chrome and
 * owns nothing but prose, tables, diagrams, and code blocks.
 *
 * The typography is deliberately document-like rather than product-like: the
 * reader's own sans-serif stack, numbered sections, `##` markers on headings,
 * and plain rules instead of cards, badges, or callouts. It should read as a
 * rendered markdown file, not a landing page.
 *
 * Snippets come from `src/data/angadrive.ts` so they can be reviewed as code
 * rather than markup.
 */
import CodeBlock from './CodeBlock.astro';
import { SNIPPETS } from '../data/angadrive';
---

<article class="report">
	<section id="brief" aria-labelledby="h-brief">
		<h2 id="h-brief"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">##</span> The brief</h2>

		<p>
			AngaDriveV3 is a remote file host, which is basically the part of Google Drive that
			stores and serves bytes, except it's one Go binary that also ships its own client. It does the
			things you'd expect: accounts, folders nested as deep as you want, sharing, conversion between
			media formats, thumbnails, bulk operations, and importing a GitHub repository as a folder tree.
			None of that is particularly hard, and realistically you could get a working version of it in a
			weekend.
		</p>

		<p>
			What took the time was making every operation cheap. The whole architecture is really a series
			of answers to one question: <em>what work can I move out of the request path?</em> The README
			puts it as "a specific focus on performance and scalability", and I think the product is what
			happens when you take that literally instead of treating it as a tagline.
		</p>

		<p>
			That shows up as four structural decisions rather than a long list of micro-optimisations. The
			database keeps its hot working set in RAM and writes through to SQLite. One persistent
			WebSocket carries every state change in both directions. Files live in a single
			content-addressed directory, so identical bytes are only ever stored once. And a bounded worker
			pool absorbs transcoding, thumbnailing and repository import, which is why an upload burst
			never competes with a page load for CPU.
		</p>

		<p>
			The live instance runs on a single Raspberry Pi and currently reports 172 registered users,
			4,132 hosted files, roughly a thousand state-change pulses a day, and 200 ms at P95 against
			750 ms at P99. Those numbers describe one deployment on one board, not a benchmark of the code
			on arbitrary hardware, and I think that distinction matters more than the numbers themselves.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="stack" aria-labelledby="h-stack">
		<h2 id="h-stack"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">##</span> Stack</h2>

		<p>
			Every choice below was made for a reason that shows up later in the architecture. Nothing here
			is a default I just went along with.
		</p>

		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th scope="col">Layer</th>
					<th scope="col">Choice</th>
					<th scope="col">Why it is here</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr>
					<td>Backend</td>
					<td>Go + gin</td>
					<td>
						Compiled, small resident set, real concurrency primitives. The whole server is one
						statically linked binary with no runtime to install.
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Transport</td>
					<td>gorilla/websocket</td>
					<td>
						Nearly every state change travels over one persistent socket instead of a request
						per action.
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Storage</td>
					<td>SQLite via gorm</td>
					<td>
						A file, not a service. There is no network hop between the application and its
						database, and the schema migrates itself at boot.
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Working set</td>
					<td>in-RAM indexes</td>
					<td>
						Hot metadata and every relationship lives in Go maps and sets; SQLite is the
						durable record behind it.
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Client</td>
					<td>SolidJS + Vite</td>
					<td>
						Fine-grained reactivity with no virtual DOM, so the bundle stays small and updates
						touch only the nodes that changed.
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Media</td>
					<td>ffmpeg, imaging, go-fitz</td>
					<td>
						Transcoding, thumbnails, and PDF page rendering, all invoked only from background
						workers.
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Assets</td>
					<td>brotli + gzip</td>
					<td>
						Every static file is compressed once at startup and served from memory with an
						encoding negotiated per request.
					</td>
				</tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>

		<p>
			One omission is deliberate. There's no Redis, no message queue, and no object store, because
			every component that would normally be a separate process is either a Go data structure or a
			goroutine. That's the whole reason the deployment story is a single command, and honestly it's
			the part I'm most pleased with.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="architecture" aria-labelledby="h-architecture">
		<h2 id="h-architecture"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">##</span> Architecture</h2>

		<p>
			The request path has three tiers: the browser, the Go process, and durable state. The browser
			holds one bundle and one socket. The Go process holds the in-RAM indexes, the SQLite handle,
			and the worker pool. Durable state is a database file plus a content-addressed upload
			directory.
		</p>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.architecture} lang="plaintext" filename="data flow" />

		<p>
			Two things in that diagram matter more than the rest. The first is that the WebSocket is
			<em>bidirectional</em>, so the client sends commands and the server pushes state changes nobody
			asked for. The second is that uploads and asset downloads deliberately bypass the socket and
			travel over plain HTTP, because a 7 MB chunk has no business being in a JSON frame and a range
			request for a video needs the browser's own machinery.
		</p>

		<p>Configuration is entirely environmental, which means there's no config file to keep in sync:</p>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.envExample} lang="bash" filename="shell" />

		<p>
			<code>WEB_URL</code> is doing double duty here: it's the origin the client is served from, and
			the only origin allowed to open the socket. <code>ASSETS_URL</code> exists so that an HTML or
			SVG file someone uploaded gets served from a host that shares no cookies, no storage, and no
			origin with the application, which is a security boundary rather than a performance trick.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="socket" aria-labelledby="h-socket">
		<h2 id="h-socket">
			<span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">##</span> One socket instead of a request per action
		</h2>

		<p>
			A conventional client does a lot of small round trips. It lists files, then fetches each
			collection's metadata, then polls for changes, and each of those spends a TLS handshake's worth
			of latency on headers, cookies and routing before it does any actual work. AngaDrive collapses
			all of that into a single upgrade at page load and a dispatch table after it.
		</p>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.dispatch} lang="go" filename="requestHandler/dispatch.go" />

		<p>
			Roughly two dozen message types share one connection, and each frame is a JSON envelope with a
			type and a payload. The consequence is a different data ownership model, because once the
			connection stays open the server can notify instead of waiting to be asked. That's what makes
			the pulse system possible: when a file is uploaded, renamed, converted or deleted, the server
			pushes an update to every connection that should see it, and the client applies the change to
			local state without ever refetching a listing.
		</p>

		<p>
			Connection bookkeeping is where this design can go wrong, and the failure mode is subtle. One
			slow socket must not stall the others. So fan-out takes a snapshot of the recipient set under a
			read lock, releases it, and then writes to each connection in its own goroutine holding only
			that connection's write mutex. A blocked write costs one goroutine instead of the whole
			broadcast, which is the difference between a slow client and a dead server.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="database" aria-labelledby="h-database">
		<h2 id="h-database"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">##</span> The database layer</h2>

		<p>
			SQLite is the durable record, but almost nothing reads it on the hot path. At startup the
			server loads the metadata into Go data structures, and from then on a read is a lock
			acquisition and a map lookup. Writes go to SQLite and to the in-memory index in the same call,
			so the two never diverge for longer than the duration of one function.
		</p>

		<h3>Indexes, not a cache</h3>

		<p>
			The vocabulary matters here, because this isn't a cache with a TTL sitting in front of a
			database. It's the primary read path, and SQLite is the write-ahead log that survives restarts.
			There's no invalidation problem because nothing is ever stale, since every mutation updates RAM
			synchronously.
		</p>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.caches} lang="go" filename="database/caches.go" />

		<p>
			Six indexes, each with its own mutex, and the sets store <code>map[string]struct{}</code> so
			membership costs no allocation. Splitting into six rather than one is what keeps contention
			low. Listing a user's drive touches <code>UserFiles</code>, checking what's inside a folder
			touches <code>CollectionFiles</code>, and resolving a file's metadata touches
			<code>FileCache</code>. Those are different mutexes, so readers on different paths don't queue
			behind each other.
		</p>

		<h3>Warm at boot</h3>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.loadCache} lang="go" filename="database/load.go" />

		<p>
			Eight loaders in parallel, one pass each, joined before the server accepts traffic. The whole
			database is in memory before the first request lands.
		</p>

		<h3>Reads: cache first, populate behind</h3>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.getFile} lang="go" filename="database/file_fetch.go" />

		<p>
			The asymmetry is the point. The read lock is held only long enough to copy the value out, and
			the expensive repair happens in a goroutine that takes the write lock. A cold key costs one
			query once, not once per reader that arrives before the repair lands, and because the repair
			re-reads under the write lock it can't clobber a concurrent mutation.
		</p>

		<h3>Writes: through, not behind</h3>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.insert} lang="go" filename="database/insert.go" />

		<p>
			Returning from <code>Insert</code> means every subsequent read observes the row, so there's no
			window where the UI and the database disagree. That's the trade the whole design rests on. A
			write-through index is more expensive on the write path than a fire-and-forget one, and it's
			the right call here because writes are rare relative to reads in a file host. A drive gets
			listed far more often than it gets modified.
		</p>

		<p>
			Deletes follow the same shape but do more work. They remove the row, invalidate every
			relationship edge, then recompute the sizes of the affected collections and their ancestors,
			which is where the next section starts to matter.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="locks" aria-labelledby="h-locks">
		<h2 id="h-locks">
			<span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">##</span> Lock discipline as a correctness invariant
		</h2>

		<p>
			Six indexes means a read can legitimately need three of them at once, and that's exactly where
			concurrent code goes wrong in ways tests rarely catch. Two things make it tractable. Locks are
			always acquired in one documented order, and any helper that requires locks to already be held
			checks that at runtime.
		</p>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.lockOrder} lang="go" filename="database/size.go" />

		<p>
			Imposing a global order removes the possibility of a cycle, which removes deadlock by
			construction instead of by careful reasoning about every call site. The naming convention
			reinforces it too: functions that require the caller to hold locks are called
			<code>unsafe*</code>, and the exported functions acquire whatever the unsafe ones need.
		</p>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.guaranteeLocks} lang="go" filename="database/caches.go" />

		<p>
			<code>TryLock</code> only succeeds if nobody holds the lock, which for a lock the caller was
			supposed to hold means the contract was violated. So that check is a development-time
			assertion with a production-time cost of one atomic operation, and it turns the most common
			concurrency bug (an <code>unsafe</code> helper called from a new site that forgot its lock)
			into an immediate panic in the test suite rather than a data race that surfaces months later as
			a wrong file size.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="relationships" aria-labelledby="h-relationships">
		<h2 id="h-relationships"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">##</span> Relationships in RAM</h2>

		<p>
			The object model is small. A file has an owner and a size. A collection has a name, an editor
			list and a size. Membership is a set of edges, collection to file and collection to collection.
			In a normalised SQL schema both of those are joins, and a nested folder view becomes a
			recursive query. Here they're two more maps.
		</p>

		<p>
			<code>CollectionFiles[collectionID]</code> is a <code>FileSet</code> of file directories, and
			<code>CollectionFolders[collectionID]</code> is a <code>CollectionSet</code> of child
			collection IDs. Listing a folder is <code>Keys()</code> on a set, so no query is issued at all.
			The normalised tables still exist and are still written, because they're what lets the indexes
			be rebuilt at boot and what a future reader would query directly.
		</p>

		<h3>Sizes, with cycles and double counting</h3>

		<p>
			A folder's size is the sum of its files plus the sizes of its children, recursively. That's a
			graph traversal, and the graph isn't necessarily a tree, since the same sub-folder can appear
			under two parents and a cycle is possible. Both are handled explicitly rather than assumed
			away.
		</p>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.collectionSize} lang="go" filename="database/size.go" />

		<p>
			Two seen-sets: one prevents infinite recursion through the folder graph, the other counts a
			file once even if it's reachable twice. The second one matters more than it looks. A file
			placed in two sub-collections is still one file occupying one quantum of disk, and counting it
			twice would overstate the size of the parent and eventually the size reported on the dashboard.
			Sizes get recomputed on mutation and propagated up the ancestor chain, so the stored value is a
			read rather than a calculation.
		</p>

		<p>
			There's a useful side effect to all of this. Because <code>Sha256sum</code> is stored on every
			row and the physical filename is derived from it, "is this file already on disk?" becomes an
			indexed question instead of a hash of every byte uploaded. Deduplication turns into a lookup.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="uploads" aria-labelledby="h-uploads">
		<h2 id="h-uploads"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">##</span> Uploads</h2>

		<p>
			A single request carrying a 1 GB body is a bad idea. You get no progress, no resume, no
			parallelism, and a server that has to either buffer the whole thing or fail. So uploads are
			chunked at 7 MB, compressed in the browser, and sent with a small number of chunks in flight.
		</p>

		<CodeBlock
			code={SNIPPETS.uploadClient}
			lang="typescript"
			filename="web/src/pages/MyDrive/shared/uploadChunks.ts"
		/>

		<p>
			Two constants are doing the real work. Six chunks in flight per file keeps the pipe full
			without making a single upload look like a burst, and three files at once respects the
			browser's per-origin connection budget. Together they mean a batch of large files saturates the
			link without any single response becoming the bottleneck.
		</p>

		<p>
			Each chunk is written to a temporary file keyed by upload id and index, and an inactivity timer
			reclaims abandoned uploads, so a closed tab doesn't leave 600 MB of orphans on disk.
		</p>

		<h3>Finalisation</h3>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.uploadServer} lang="go" filename="endpoints/upload.go" />

		<p>
			The ordering here is deliberate. Hashing happens during assembly rather than in a second pass,
			because the file may be large and the bytes are already in the page cache. The finished file is
			moved with <code>rename</code>, which is atomic on POSIX, so a reader can never observe a
			half-written upload at its final path. And because the destination filename is the SHA-256, two
			users uploading the same file converge on the same inode. The second upload is a rename over an
			identical file, and the database gains a row without gaining storage.
		</p>

		<p>
			The visible latency of an upload isn't the time it takes to write bytes, it's the time until
			the file appears. Because the pulse is emitted from the insert path, the card shows up as soon
			as the rename and row are done, while thumbnailing and any requested conversion happen
			afterwards in the runner pool. That's the trick that makes uploads feel instant even when the
			work behind them isn't.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="runners" aria-labelledby="h-runners">
		<h2 id="h-runners"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">##</span> Background compute</h2>

		<p>
			Transcoding a video is unbounded work. An arbitrary file on an arbitrary machine can occupy a
			core for minutes, and doing that inside an HTTP handler means a request burst can starve the
			server of CPU while the queue grows without limit. The runners package is the answer: a small,
			generic, bounded job system.
		</p>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.runnerManager} lang="go" filename="runners/runners.go" />

		<p>
			Three properties matter. It's <strong>bounded</strong>, because the semaphore caps concurrent
			workers at <code>MAX_RUNNER_COUNT</code> (default 5), so a queue of forty conversions still
			leaves cores for request handling. It's <strong>non-blocking</strong>, because
			<code>Submit</code> uses a <code>select</code> with a <code>default</code>, so a full queue
			returns an error immediately instead of parking the caller. Backpressure is a rejected request,
			not a stall. And it's <strong>idempotent</strong>, because <code>LoadOrStore</code> on the
			job's content hash means submitting the same video twice queues it once.
		</p>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.runnerWorker} lang="go" filename="runners/runners.go" />

		<p>
			Each job runs in its own goroutine but has to hold a semaphore slot, which is released on the
			way out. The <code>Notifier</code> interface is the seam that keeps this decoupled: runners
			report completion through it and the WebSocket layer supplies the implementation. The runner
			framework has no import of the socket layer at all, which means it would keep working if the
			transport changed tomorrow.
		</p>

		<h3>Previews for a library that already exists</h3>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.backfill} lang="go" filename="runners/backfill.go" />

		<p>
			Backfill only ever runs when the queue is empty and nothing is executing, so it can never delay
			a user-initiated job. It's a small piece of scheduling with an outsized effect on perceived
			quality. A library uploaded before preview generation existed slowly acquires thumbnails in the
			background, without anyone waiting and without any job competing with a real request. On a
			machine that's genuinely idle it works through the backlog, and on a busy one it makes no
			progress at all, which is exactly what you want.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="previews" aria-labelledby="h-previews">
		<h2 id="h-previews"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">##</span> Previews</h2>

		<p>
			Every preview type follows the same contract. It's keyed by the content hash, generated at most
			once, written atomically, and never larger than the source file. The differences are only in
			how the bytes get produced.
		</p>

		<h3>Images</h3>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.imagePreview} lang="go" filename="previews/image_preview.go" />

		<p>
			Three failure modes are handled explicitly. Photos straight off a phone carry an EXIF
			orientation tag and no rotation in their pixels, so a naive decode renders them sideways, which
			is why the tag gets applied during decode. Transparency is measured rather than assumed from
			the format, because a PNG can be fully opaque and a JPEG never has alpha, and encoding an
			opaque image as PNG would multiply its size for nothing. And a preview larger than its source
			is pointless, so in that case the original gets copied instead.
		</p>

		<p>
			SVG is a special case. It's served as-is below a size threshold rather than rasterised, because
			the browser renders it natively and a bitmap would lose resolution while gaining bytes.
		</p>

		<h3>PDF</h3>

		<p>
			Page one is rendered through MuPDF bindings (<code>go-fitz</code>), resized to the same 512 px
			ceiling, and encoded as JPEG. PDF pages are opaque by definition, so the PNG branch would never
			apply anyway.
		</p>

		<h3>Video</h3>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.videoPreview} lang="go" filename="previews/video_preview.go" />

		<p>
			The format choice is what makes this cheap. A GIF is limited to 256 colours, which is visible
			on any real footage, and its inter-frame compression is primitive. Animated WebP gives full
			colour and a modern encoder, and at quality 80 with maximum compression effort it produces a
			preview roughly an order of magnitude smaller than the equivalent GIF. Fifteen seconds at 24
			fps and 350 px is the size budget, and long clips get time-compressed with
			<code>setpts</code> rather than cut, so the preview represents the whole video instead of its
			first few moments.
		</p>

		<p>
			Corrupt input is expected, because a <code>.mp4</code> that isn't one or a truncated download
			will happen. Encoding failures write a minimal valid WebP instead of leaving nothing behind.
			That distinction matters more than it sounds, because "generated, but empty" is cacheable and
			stops a retry loop, whereas a missing file would be retried on every single request.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="frontend" aria-labelledby="h-frontend">
		<h2 id="h-frontend"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">##</span> The client</h2>

		<p>
			The client is a SolidJS single-page app. Solid's model is compile-time reactivity, so
			components run once and updates mutate the exact DOM nodes bound to a changed signal. There's
			no virtual DOM and no diffing pass, which is why a file grid with a thousand cards stays
			responsive while pulses are arriving.
		</p>

		<h3>Nothing loads until it is about to be seen</h3>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.observer} lang="tsx" filename="web/src/components/FilePreview.tsx" />

		<p>
			Two layers make this work. The observer means a folder with 400 images issues a handful of
			preview requests rather than 400. The <code>loadedFiles</code> set persists across navigation,
			so returning to a folder re-renders previews from the browser cache instead of asking the
			server again. The same observer pattern drives infinite scroll in the grid, where a sentinel at
			the bottom extends the rendered window by a page at a time.
		</p>

		<h3>The server is the only writer</h3>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.pulse} lang="tsx" filename="web/src/library/functions.tsx" />

		<p>
			Three cases: insert, delete, replace. The client never refetches a listing in response to a
			change. Actions are optimistic, so a rename applies locally at once, and the pulse is what
			reconciles the client with the authoritative record, including on other devices and other tabs.
			That's why the protocol carries an explicit insert/delete/replace distinction instead of a
			generic "list changed" event. The client can apply a surgical update rather than rebuilding its
			state.
		</p>

		<h3>Recovery</h3>

		<p>
			The socket reconnects 300 ms after a close, and before it does, every handler on the old
			instance gets nulled out. That detail prevents the classic leak where a stale socket's
			<code>onmessage</code> keeps firing into a component tree that's already been replaced.
			Credentials in storage are replayed on open, so a reconnect restores an authenticated session
			without a login round trip.
		</p>

		<p>
			Charts are configured with zero animation duration. A real-time CPU graph animating every
			update spends its time interpolating rather than showing you the truth, and rendering each
			sample instantly makes the panel read as an instrument instead of a decoration.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="assets" aria-labelledby="h-assets">
		<h2 id="h-assets"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">##</span> Build and serve</h2>

		<p>
			Static assets get the same treatment as database reads. The expensive work happens once, and
			the request path does as little as possible.
		</p>

		<h3>Rebuild only when the sources changed</h3>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.frontendHash} lang="go" filename="endpoints/frontend.go" />

		<p>
			Hashing relative paths and contents means adding, deleting and editing a file all invalidate
			the cached build, which is the three cases a timestamp misses. The binary owns its client. On
			first run it builds the frontend and caches the result, and on later runs it hashes the
			sources, compares, and skips the build entirely. That's what makes <code>go run main.go</code>
			a complete deployment, and it removes the usual class of bug where a stale bundle gets served
			against a newer backend.
		</p>

		<h3>Compress once, negotiate per request</h3>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.cachedFile} lang="go" filename="endpoints/frontend_cache.go" />

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.negotiate} lang="go" filename="endpoints/frontend_cache.go" />

		<p>
			Compressing at the maximum level is a bad idea per request and a very good one per deployment.
			Brotli level 11 on a JavaScript bundle takes seconds, and doing that on every request would be
			a denial of service. Doing it once, holding all three variants in memory, and answering with
			whichever the <code>Accept-Encoding</code> header prefers means the client gets the smallest
			possible payload and the server does a map lookup. The <code>Vary</code> header is mandatory
			here, or an intermediary cache will happily hand a Brotli body to a client that only
			understands gzip.
		</p>

		<p>
			The build itself can inline CSS and JavaScript into a single HTML document, which trades
			caching granularity for one round trip on first load. For a UI that's then entirely
			socket-driven, that's usually the right side of the trade.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="deploy" aria-labelledby="h-deploy">
		<h2 id="h-deploy"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">##</span> Deployment</h2>

		<p>
			Self-hosting is three dependencies and one command: Go, Bun for the build, and ffmpeg for
			media. The server creates its own directory tree, migrates its own schema, and builds its own
			client.
		</p>

		<CodeBlock
			code={`git clone git@github.com:Anga205/AngaDriveV3.git --depth 1
cd AngaDriveV3
export WEB_URL=drive.example.com ASSETS_URL=i.example.com GIN_MODE=release
go run main.go`}
			lang="bash"
			filename="shell"
		/>

		<p>
			Schema changes are applied at boot, including a completed migration of file hashes from MD5 to
			SHA-256 that renames existing objects rather than re-uploading them. That's the kind of thing a
			self-hosted project has to get right, because an operator who upgraded in place shouldn't have
			to run a manual script afterwards.
		</p>

		<h3>Two origins, one application</h3>

		<p>
			Serving user content from <code>ASSETS_URL</code> is a security boundary, not a performance
			trick. An uploaded HTML or SVG file served from the app's own origin could read its storage and
			make authenticated requests. From a second origin it's inert: no cookies, no storage, no
			same-origin fetch. CORS on the asset host is restricted to the web origin so legitimate
			previews still load.
		</p>

		<h3>Continuous integration</h3>

		<CodeBlock code={SNIPPETS.ci} lang="yaml" filename=".github/workflows/integration.yml" />

		<p>
			The frontend and server are built once and shared as artifacts, and the tests then fan out
			across a matrix against a real server and a real socket. They're integration tests in the
			strict sense: they start the built binary, connect a real WebSocket, upload real chunks, and
			assert on the pulses that come back. Running them with <code>SAVE_DRIVE_RAM=true</code> is
			deliberate, since that setting trades a little latency for a smaller resident set, so using it
			in CI exercises the alternate code path and keeps the runners from competing with the
			assertions.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="numbers" aria-labelledby="h-numbers">
		<h2 id="h-numbers"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">##</span> Production numbers</h2>

		<p>
			The live instance is the only benchmark that exists, and it's a single machine. Read these as
			characteristics of the design under real load rather than as a comparison against a
			hyperscaler.
		</p>

		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th scope="col">Metric</th>
					<th scope="col">Value</th>
					<th scope="col">Note</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr>
					<td>Hardware</td>
					<td>1 Raspberry Pi</td>
					<td>one node, no replicas, no managed services</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Users</td>
					<td>172</td>
					<td>registered accounts</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Files</td>
					<td>4,132</td>
					<td>hosted, after deduplication</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Activity</td>
					<td>~1,000/day</td>
					<td>state-change pulses emitted</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Latency</td>
					<td>200 ms P95</td>
					<td>750 ms at P99</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Memory</td>
					<td>under 1 GB</td>
					<td>typical, with headroom for one transcode</td>
				</tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>

		<p>
			The memory figure is the one that constrains the design. Holding every index in RAM is only
			viable because the indexes are compact: a set of file directories and a set of collection IDs
			per user, not whole rows. Four thousand files of metadata is a small fraction of a gigabyte,
			which is what makes the expensive part of the design affordable in the first place.
			<code>SAVE_DRIVE_RAM</code> exists for the operator whose library is large enough that it
			isn't.
		</p>

		<p>
			Latency at the tail is dominated by operations that are intentionally not on the hot path. A
			P99 of 750 ms on a Pi is the cost of a conversion or a large chunk finalising, not of listing a
			folder. The median request doesn't do I/O at all.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="reading" aria-labelledby="h-reading">
		<h2 id="h-reading"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">##</span> Further reading</h2>

		<p>
			The source is the complete specification and the live instance is the running one. Both are
			worth more than any summary, including this one, so if any of the above was interesting, the
			code is where the details actually live.
		</p>

